## 📄 Abstract
This preprint provides a structural engineering and quantitative logistics analysis of the prophetic temple architecture described in Ezekiel 40–48. Rather than focusing on traditional eschatological frameworks, this paper evaluates the layout through the parameters of applied physics, load-bearing statics, fluid dynamics, and material procurement constraints. We analyze the specialized cantilevered ledges engineered for the priests' side chambers, the structural load distribution of the six 90-foot-tall gatehouses, and the mass transport calculations required to construct a 17-acre sanctuary using ancient masonry methodologies. The findings demonstrate that the layout features sophisticated structural mechanics designed to isolate and protect sacred space while exposing a logistical paradox that transcends ancient labor and topographical capacities.

