## 2. Structural Mechanics of the Cantilevered Ledges
Ezekiel 41:5–7 dictates an advanced framing method for the 90 side chambers encircling the outer sanctuary walls. Traditional ancient Near Eastern masonry typically carved pockets directly into primary structural load-bearing walls to recess timber joists for secondary floors. This common practice creates deep structural stress concentrations and significantly weakens the primary wall over long time horizons. 

In contrast, the Ezekiel blueprint enforces a stepped foundation on the outer face of the sanctuary wall. As the main wall extends vertically, its cross-sectional thickness decreases systematically at each story level, forming exposed structural ledges.

From a statics perspective, this configuration establishes an independent, cantilevered load distribution. The weight of the three-tiered chambers rests entirely upon the external stepped buttresses. Consequently, the secondary framing members never pierce or compromise the core sanctuary walls. The inner masonry housing the Holy of Holies is shielded from all direct shear stress, bending moments, and structural vibrations induced by the surrounding living spaces.
