## 3. Mass Transport and Quantitative Labor Logistics
To evaluate the structural feasibility of the layout, we model the total required material mass and manual labor hours using the ancient construction baselines of the First Temple. King Solomon managed a sweeping mobilization of approximately 183,300 workers over a 7-year tenure (~3 billion labor hours) to erect a structure with a fraction of Ezekiel's square footage. 

Due to the 17-acre continuous courtyard floor, six staggering 90-foot multi-story gatehouses, and extensive stone gallery spaces, the total volume of dressed ashlar masonry required for the Ezekiel layout scales exponentially to an estimated 15 to 20 billion manual labor hours.

During the Neo-Babylonian era in which the text was generated, the surviving regional workforce was depleted and entirely incapable of funding or executing a project of this magnitude. Furthermore, the 25,000-cubit sacred allotment layout assumes a complete topographical releveling of the Judean mountain ranges, establishing a clear logistical paradox: a layout engineered with perfect architectural accuracy that intentionally completely overwhelms available human economies and natural geographic constraints.

